  <section id="essentials">
    <h2>The essentials: be seen, be able to float, be able to get out</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need much to swim in a river, but a few items directly affect your safety. This page is a practical list, written from the kind of advice published by outdoor swimming organisations and the RNLI rather than from product testing: we have not tested the products named in the general categories below, and we are not recommending specific brands.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tow float.</strong> A brightly coloured inflatable float attached to your waist. It makes you visible to boats, anglers and paddlers, gives you something to rest on if you tire, and can hold a dry bag with your phone and keys. It is not a lifejacket, and it will not save you if you are unconscious.</li>
      <li><strong>Brightly coloured swim hat.</strong> Another visibility aid, and it keeps a little heat in.</li>
      <li><strong>Suitable swimwear and a way to carry your belongings.</strong> Pack so you can find warm dry clothes quickly. A dry bag helps.</li>
      <li><strong>A friend.</strong> Not an item, but the single most useful safety measure. Swimming with someone means help is on hand.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="warmth">
    <h2>Staying warm in cold water</h2>
    <p>
      UK rivers are cold for most of the year, and cold water shock is a real danger below about 15°C. Neoprene gloves, socks and a hat reduce heat loss from your extremities, which are the first parts to feel the cold. Many swimmers add a wetsuit in spring and autumn; others prefer to swim skins and build up tolerance gradually. Either way, shorter swims are better than longer ones when the water is cold. If you start to shiver hard, lose dexterity or feel confused, get out.
    </p>
    <p>
      Entering slowly, controlling your breathing and acclimatising over weeks is more important than any single piece of kit. For the water-quality and cold-shock basics, read <a href="/blog/is-it-safe-to-swim-in-the-river-today/">our river swimming safety guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="after">
    <h2>After the swim: warming up safely</h2>
    <p>
      Your temperature can keep dropping after you leave the water (so-called afterdrop), so most of the discomfort and risk comes in the first 20 minutes on the bank. Dry yourself, get layers on your torso first, add a hat, and have a warm drink and something to eat. A large changing robe or dryrobe-style coat makes this easier, especially where there is nowhere to change, but a big towel and warm clothes do the same job. Avoid a hot shower straight away if you are very cold, as rapid rewarming can make you dizzy.
    </p>
  </section>

