  <section id="nice">
    <h2>Nice to have</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Water shoes for stony or uncertain river beds, which also help on slippery banks.</li>
      <li>A thermos with a hot drink.</li>
      <li>A thermometer so you know the water temperature before getting in.</li>
      <li>A waterproof phone pouch, so you can call for help if you need to.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="skip">
    <h2>What you can skip</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need expensive specialist kit to start. A swimsuit, a towel, warm clothes, a hat and a friend are enough for a short swim on a warm day in a safe, easy spot. Add equipment gradually, as you learn what you actually use. Be wary of anything marketed as a safety device that cannot do what it promises, such as relying on a tow float as a flotation aid in strong currents.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kit-list">
    <h2>A simple kit list</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Swimwear and a bright swim hat</li>
      <li>Tow float and dry bag</li>
      <li>Neoprene gloves, socks and a hat for cold water</li>
      <li>Big towel or changing robe, plus warm layers</li>
      <li>Hot drink and a snack</li>
      <li>Phone in a waterproof pouch, and someone who knows your plan</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Read <a href="/blog/wild-swimming-uk-rivers-guide/">our full guide to wild swimming in UK rivers</a> for places to swim and general advice.
    </p>
  </section>

